## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over time with multiple constraints: multiple transactions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Two DP states: cash (not holding) and hold (holding).
- Transitions model buy/sell with transaction count t.
- Base cases initialize day 0; backtracking recovers trade days.
- Space optimization possible to O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfit(prices, k){
  const n = prices.length;
  if (n===0 || k===0) return 0;
  const cash = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const hold = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) cash[0][t] = (t===0)?0:-1e15;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++){
      cash[i][t] = cash[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) cash[i][t] = Math.max(cash[i][t], hold[i-1][t] + prices[i-1]);
      hold[i][t] = hold[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i][t], cash[i-1][t-1] - prices[i-1]);
    }
  }
  let best = 0;
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) best = Math.max(best, cash[n][t]);
  return best;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the exact trade days from the DP table?
- How does the solution change if k >= n/2 (unlimited transactions)?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (distinct character count). It's concrete, implementable, and scales well to typical interview constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and partition count
- Precomputing distinct character counts for all substrings (O(n²) with two pointers)
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartitionDistinct(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      seen.add(s[j]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t - 1] + dist[j][i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const partitions = [];
  let [i, t] = [n, k];
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = prev[i][t];
    partitions.unshift([j, i - 1]);
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New DP angle focusing on subarray range gain with exact k partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP partitioning
- Precomputing range max/min
- Reconstruction through pointers
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGainKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const gain = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = A[l], mx = A[l];
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[r]);
      gain[l][r] = mx - mn;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      take[i][t] = take[i-1][t];
      if(t>0){
        for(let l=1;l<=i;l++){
          const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + gain[l-1][i-1];
          if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = l-1; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const lminus1 = take[i][t];
    const l = lminus1 + 1;
    res.unshift([l, i]);
    i = lminus1; t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct blocks?
- How would the solution change if subarrays must have at least a fixed length L?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic programming with a non-adjacency constraint and explicit reconstruction of the deletion indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index to enforce non-adjacency
- Exact deletions constraint (k) and reconstruction
- O(nk) time and O(nk) space (can optimize to O(k) with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumAfterDeletions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const keep = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const del = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  keep[0][0] = A[0];
  if (k >= 1) del[0][1] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      keep[i][t] = Math.max(keep[i-1][t], del[i-1][t]);
      if (keep[i][t] > NEG/2) keep[i][t] += A[i];
      if (t > 0) del[i][t] = keep[i-1][t-1];
    }
  }
  const end = Math.max(keep[n-1][k], del[n-1][k]);
  // Backtracking to collect deleted indices would be added here
  return {maxSum: end, deletedIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact deleted indices efficiently? 
- Can you reduce space to O(k) and still reconstruct? 
- How does the approach change if adjacent deletions are allowed at a cost?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests precise DP construction for selecting k disjoint subarrays with per-subarray penalty, a common optimization variant.

## Key Concepts
- Disjoint subarray DP with two states
- Transition derivation for starting/continuing a subarray
- Space/time trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  best[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], t>0 ? best[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] : NEG);
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t], end[i][t] - c);
    }
  }
  return {value: best[n][k], subs: null};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct the actual subarrays from the DP?
- How to optimize memory to O(k)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a non-trivial partition DP with a substring cost that requires precomputation and reconstruction, combining both combinatorial choices and an aggregate cost function.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions with a cost function over substrings
- Efficient precomputation of substring distinct counts
- Backtracking reconstruction of cut points
- Time/space trade-offs and memory optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: precompute cost[p][i], then DP with backpointers
const n = s.length;
const INF = 1e9;
const cost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
for (let p = 0; p < n; p++) {
  const seen = new Array(26).fill(false);
  let distinct = 0;
  for (let i = p; i < n; i++) {
    const idx = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
    if (!seen[idx]) { seen[idx] = true; distinct++; }
    cost[p][i] = distinct;
  }
}
const kCap = Math.min(k, n);
const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(kCap+1).fill(INF));
const prv = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(kCap+1).fill(-1));
dp[0][0] = 0;
